WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.610
- For the first time in nearly 30 years,

00:00:01.610 --> 00:00:03.430
a person was killed on a film set

00:00:03.430 --> 00:00:05.060
after being accidentally shot.

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:07.169
Is Alec Baldwin going to jail?

00:00:07.169 --> 00:00:09.810
(upbeat music)

00:00:09.810 --> 00:00:11.460
The camera crew was setting up for a shot

00:00:11.460 --> 00:00:14.530
when Alec Baldwin put his
hand on the Colt .45 revolver

00:00:14.530 --> 00:00:15.507
in its holster and said,

00:00:15.507 --> 00:00:17.727
"So I guess I'm gonna take
this out, pull it out,

00:00:17.727 --> 00:00:19.342
"and go, 'Bang.'"
(gun firing)

00:00:19.342 --> 00:00:20.910
What followed was shock and confusion.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.020
As Baldwin pulled the
gun out of its holster,

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:24.850
a lead bullet exploded from the muzzle,

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.130
hitting Director of
Photography Halyna Hutchins,

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:28.110
who tumbled backwards.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:29.740
Director Joel Souza also hit the ground

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.580
when the bullet exited Hutchins's body

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:33.317
and embedded in his shoulder.

00:00:33.317 --> 00:00:34.710
"What the F just happened?"

00:00:34.710 --> 00:00:37.660
That was the question Baldwin
repeated over and over again.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:39.470
A 911 caller alerted the dispatcher

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:40.997
that there was trouble on set.

00:00:40.997 --> 00:00:42.870
- [Script Supervisor]
Bonanza Creek Ranch just had

00:00:42.870 --> 00:00:47.550
two people accidentally shot
on a movie set by a prop gun.

00:00:47.550 --> 00:00:49.110
We need help immediately.

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:50.740
Bonanza Creek Ranch. Come on.

00:00:50.740 --> 00:00:52.437
- [Dispatcher] So, was it
loaded with a real bullet?

00:00:52.437 --> 00:00:55.248
- [Script Supervisor] I don't,
I cannot tell you that."

00:00:55.248 --> 00:00:57.180
- Hutchins's death sent shockwaves

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.100
through the entertainment industry,

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.830
which had just averted a crippling strike

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:01.110
by film industry workers.

00:01:01.110 --> 00:01:03.410
Hollywood TV and film
crews were still reeling

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.810
from a 2014 train accident
that killed Sarah Jones

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.480
and injured eight others.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:09.690
Her death, and now the
loss of Halyna Hutchins,

00:01:09.690 --> 00:01:11.740
are raising serious questions
about workplace safety

00:01:11.740 --> 00:01:13.910
on film and TV productions.

00:01:13.910 --> 00:01:15.930
So today we're gonna talk
about who was responsible

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:17.820
for what happened on the set of "Rust."

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:19.730
Could there be criminal penalties?

00:01:19.730 --> 00:01:21.910
Could Alec Baldwin face
manslaughter charges?

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.130
And what about the armorer
and assistant director

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.270
who were responsible
for checking the guns?

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:28.720
Now, an important
standard disclaimer here,

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:32.010
we don't know what happened
on the set of this film.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:34.970
We don't know what actions
people took or did not take.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:36.210
A lot of that will come as a result

00:01:36.210 --> 00:01:37.310
of the criminal investigation,

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:39.190
which appears to be in process.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:41.630
So a lot of this video
is gonna be speculative

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:43.860
based on hypotheticals of
what may have happened.

00:01:43.860 --> 00:01:45.690
So, don't jump to any conclusions here.

00:01:45.690 --> 00:01:47.360
A lot of this information
is going to change

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.300
and a lot more information
is going to come to light.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:51.667
But first let's talk
about this movie itself.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:53.280
"Rust" is an upcoming western

00:01:53.280 --> 00:01:55.040
written and directed by Joel Souza.

00:01:55.040 --> 00:01:58.540
It stars Alec Baldwin, Travis
Fimmel, and Jensen Ackles.

00:01:58.540 --> 00:02:00.780
The plot follows a 13-year-old
boy who goes on the run

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:02.980
with his grandpa after the
boy is sentenced to hang

00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:04.320
for an accidental killing.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:06.870
The Los Angeles Times has the
most updated reconstruction

00:02:06.870 --> 00:02:08.600
of what happened on the "Rust" set.

00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:10.050
Their timeline is based on interviews

00:02:10.050 --> 00:02:11.450
with 14 members of the crew,

00:02:11.450 --> 00:02:13.130
official statements by
the parties involved,

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:14.970
and the search warrant affidavits.

00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:16.540
The events that led up
to the shooting started

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.360
at 6:10 a.m. on Thursday

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.960
when six members of the
camera crew walked off the set

00:02:20.960 --> 00:02:22.850
before the day's filming got started.

00:02:22.850 --> 00:02:24.840
Their resignations came
after a week of complaints

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:26.610
about unsatisfactory working conditions,

00:02:26.610 --> 00:02:28.660
including a lack of safety on set.

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:30.460
In the days before the camera crew quit,

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:32.300
Hutchins had tried to resolve
some of their complaints

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.200
by forfeiting a half day's
rental of a Technocrane,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:38.550
equipment that enables
photographers to get aerial shots.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:41.130
She hopes that by sacrificing
part of her budget,

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:42.350
the producers could spend the money

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:44.450
on closer lodging for the crew.

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:45.880
The lodging problem was so serious

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:47.160
that it became a running joke.

00:02:47.160 --> 00:02:48.837
Someone ordered custom T-shirts that read,

00:02:48.837 --> 00:02:51.740
"Error 404: Housing Not Found."

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:53.480
On Wednesday, the night
before the shooting,

00:02:53.480 --> 00:02:55.740
Jonas Huerta, the digital
utility technician,

00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:57.882
wrote an email outlining
his safety concerns:

00:02:57.882 --> 00:02:59.717
"I also feel anxious on set.

00:02:59.717 --> 00:03:02.777
"I've seen firsthand our
assistant director David Halls

00:03:02.777 --> 00:03:06.137
"rush to get shots and he
skips over important protocols.

00:03:06.137 --> 00:03:07.317
"He often rushes to shoot.

00:03:07.317 --> 00:03:08.727
"I've had more than a few occasions

00:03:08.727 --> 00:03:10.687
"where I've been close to
the weapons being fired

00:03:10.687 --> 00:03:12.317
"with no regards to my hearing.

00:03:12.317 --> 00:03:13.597
"Sometimes he rushes so quickly

00:03:13.597 --> 00:03:16.437
"that props hasn't even had
the chance to bring earplugs

00:03:16.437 --> 00:03:18.450
"and he rolls and the actors fire anyway."

00:03:18.450 --> 00:03:20.740
Just 10 hours later, Hutchins
was surprised to find

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.140
the camera crew was leaving.

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.300
As she tearfully hugged
the camera operators,

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:26.700
Line Producer Gabrielle Pickle
told the camera operators

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.910
to pick up the pace and get off the set.

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:30.830
She threatened to call security on them.

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:32.640
That's when David Halls
interrupted the scene

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:33.820
to rush Hutchins away,

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:36.370
saying that they needed
to prepare for the day.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:39.000
Baldwin's call time was 7:54 a.m.

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:40.960
At 8 a.m., the crew had a safety meeting.

00:03:40.960 --> 00:03:43.610
After a few hours, they
completed the first shot.

00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:44.950
Lunch was called.

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.330
Property master Sarah
Zachary retrieved the guns

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:49.400
that would be needed for
the scene from a truck

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:52.240
where the weapons were
stored inside a locked safe.

00:03:52.240 --> 00:03:53.730
The ammunition was also in the truck,

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.520
but had been left unsecured
on a cart during the break.

00:03:56.520 --> 00:03:58.677
Crew members told the
LA Times that Baldwin,

00:03:58.677 --> 00:04:01.000
"Was pretty concerned
about safety on the set."

00:04:01.000 --> 00:04:03.587
When Baldwin rehearsed this
shot a few days earlier,

00:04:03.587 --> 00:04:05.557
"He wanted to know where
I would be standing

00:04:05.557 --> 00:04:07.020
"when he drew his gun."

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:08.480
Apparently the camera technician told him

00:04:08.480 --> 00:04:10.640
he'd be standing in a different
spot, and he said, "Good."

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:12.680
However, before the fatal shot was fired,

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:14.630
there was apparently a
major security breach.

00:04:14.630 --> 00:04:16.100
David Halls told investigators

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:18.300
that his typical onset safety protocols

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:21.350
included him checking the
gun barrel for obstructions

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.270
before the armorer opens
up the hatch to the weapon

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:24.400
and spins the drum.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:27.150
Halls told investigators that
Reed performed a safety check

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:29.590
with a Colt .45 in front of Halls.

00:04:29.590 --> 00:04:32.070
Although he thought he saw
three rounds inside the gun,

00:04:32.070 --> 00:04:34.760
he did not check them before
taking the weapon in his hand.

00:04:34.760 --> 00:04:37.460
This is apparently a serious
breach in safety protocols,

00:04:37.460 --> 00:04:39.910
which require more than one
person to check the gun.

00:04:39.910 --> 00:04:42.300
Halls then ran through
Baldwin's blocking himself,

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:43.420
pulling the gun from the holster

00:04:43.420 --> 00:04:45.020
three times without incident.

00:04:45.020 --> 00:04:46.740
He did not, however, pull the trigger.

00:04:46.740 --> 00:04:48.640
And before Baldwin was ready to film,

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:50.010
he wanted to do a quick rehearsal

00:04:50.010 --> 00:04:52.410
while the director and
Hutchins lined up for the shot.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.510
Evidently, Halls is the person

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:54.620
who handed the gun to Baldwin,

00:04:54.620 --> 00:04:56.650
and early reports from the set indicated

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:57.910
that the Halls yelled, "Cold gun,"

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.790
before giving the weapon to Baldwin.

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.460
However, new evidence indicates
that this might not be true.

00:05:02.460 --> 00:05:04.810
Now, Halls and Reed are pointing
the finger at each other.

00:05:04.810 --> 00:05:06.650
Reed says that Halls
handed the gun to Baldwin,

00:05:06.650 --> 00:05:07.710
yelling, "Cold gun."

00:05:07.710 --> 00:05:10.590
But he claims that she was the
one who yelled, "Cold gun."

00:05:10.590 --> 00:05:12.430
Other crew members
remember Reed being the one

00:05:12.430 --> 00:05:14.240
who told Baldwin the
gun was actually cold,

00:05:14.240 --> 00:05:15.580
meaning it held blanks.

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:17.340
However, if someone had
double checked the rounds,

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:19.440
they probably would have realized
that they weren't blanks.

00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:21.160
A visual inspection would have showed

00:05:21.160 --> 00:05:23.730
that at least one lacked
the small hole or indent

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:26.120
that visually differentiates
dummies from bullets.

00:05:26.120 --> 00:05:27.310
They also would have noticed

00:05:27.310 --> 00:05:28.920
that it didn't make the signature rattling

00:05:28.920 --> 00:05:30.390
that proves that there's only BBs

00:05:30.390 --> 00:05:32.270
and no gunpowder in the dummy round.

00:05:32.270 --> 00:05:33.880
But admittedly, these are small details,

00:05:33.880 --> 00:05:36.600
which is why productions
rely on experts in firearms

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.210
rather than performers to do the checks.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.800
But the end result is that when Baldwin

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.410
started to rehearse his quick draw,

00:05:41.410 --> 00:05:43.360
he pulled the trigger and
then watched helplessly

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.230
as the bullet hit Hutchins.

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:47.210
Several reports say
that after the shooting,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:48.530
Baldwin repeatedly exclaimed

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:50.310
that he had never been given a hot gun,

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:52.440
a firearm with live rounds, on set.

00:05:52.440 --> 00:05:54.960
But who has responsibility
for gun safety on set?

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:55.830
Well, as you can imagine,

00:05:55.830 --> 00:05:57.230
the use of firearms on set is subject

00:05:57.230 --> 00:05:58.820
to stringent safety standards.

00:05:58.820 --> 00:06:00.750
According to the website
for the Santa Fe chapter

00:06:00.750 --> 00:06:03.670
of the IATSE, the union for film workers,

00:06:03.670 --> 00:06:05.950
an armorer must be on set when any weapons

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:07.169
or firearms are used.

00:06:07.169 --> 00:06:09.557
"Armorers liaise with the
director of photography

00:06:09.557 --> 00:06:11.327
"to determine which camera
angles will minimize

00:06:11.327 --> 00:06:12.517
"any risk of injury.

00:06:12.517 --> 00:06:14.517
"During rehearsals,
armorers coach the actors

00:06:14.517 --> 00:06:15.867
"in the correct use of the firearms,

00:06:15.867 --> 00:06:17.360
"explaining possible dangers."

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:18.850
And every armorer and prop expert

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.090
who has spoken since the accident

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:21.990
says that productions don't use real guns

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:23.070
with live rounds anymore,

00:06:23.070 --> 00:06:25.360
except in extremely rare circumstances.

00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:27.580
The use of live rounds would
probably need to be approved

00:06:27.580 --> 00:06:29.060
by the insurance company ahead of time.

00:06:29.060 --> 00:06:30.730
And the first news reports indicated

00:06:30.730 --> 00:06:32.990
the "Rust" set used prop
guns with dummy ammo.

00:06:32.990 --> 00:06:33.970
Sheriff Adan Mendoza

00:06:33.970 --> 00:06:35.870
and District Attorney Mary Carmack-Altwies

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.240
have now spoken about the
case for the first time.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:39.630
Although the investigation is ongoing,

00:06:39.630 --> 00:06:43.420
the DA declared that the gun
did fire a live lead round.

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:45.530
The bullet was removed from
the director's shoulder.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:46.410
This is a serious problem,

00:06:46.410 --> 00:06:49.220
since you can imagine live
rounds are rarely used

00:06:49.220 --> 00:06:50.560
on a film set.

00:06:50.560 --> 00:06:52.100
Anonymous crew members told the media

00:06:52.100 --> 00:06:54.350
that people were shooting
tin cans with prop guns

00:06:54.350 --> 00:06:55.610
on the morning of the homicide,

00:06:55.610 --> 00:06:57.940
and there's a possibility
that the prop guns were loaded

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:00.700
with live ammo and no one
checked the guns after lunch.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:03.709
Mendoza said that the gun
was a long Colt revolver:

00:07:03.709 --> 00:07:06.067
"It's a suspected live
round that was fired,

00:07:06.067 --> 00:07:07.417
"but it did fire from the weapon

00:07:07.417 --> 00:07:09.167
"and it did cause the injury.

00:07:09.167 --> 00:07:11.300
"That would lead us to believe
that it was a live round."

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:12.497
Mendoza confirmed that,

00:07:12.497 --> 00:07:15.350
"There was a complacency
towards weapons safety,"

00:07:15.350 --> 00:07:17.530
while the DA said that
criminal charges may be filed

00:07:17.530 --> 00:07:19.090
against the responsible parties.

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:19.960
Asked about the possibility

00:07:19.960 --> 00:07:21.933
that Alec Baldwin will
face charges, the DA said,

00:07:21.933 --> 00:07:23.950
"No one has been ruled out."

00:07:23.950 --> 00:07:25.080
Investigators have collected

00:07:25.080 --> 00:07:27.640
about 500 rounds of
ammunition, including blanks,

00:07:27.640 --> 00:07:31.320
detectives recovered two boxes
of ammo, loose ammo in boxes,

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.720
as well as a, "Fanny pack with ammo,"

00:07:33.720 --> 00:07:35.540
along with several spent casings.

00:07:35.540 --> 00:07:37.430
Evidence will be sent to the FBI crime lab

00:07:37.430 --> 00:07:39.270
in Quantico, Virginia for analysis.

00:07:39.270 --> 00:07:40.770
A prop gun is not a toy.

00:07:40.770 --> 00:07:42.320
TV and film crews typically use

00:07:42.320 --> 00:07:44.860
specially designed blank-firing firearms.

00:07:44.860 --> 00:07:47.220
These weapons cannot be
loaded with live ammunition.

00:07:47.220 --> 00:07:49.660
Prop guns add authenticity
by mimicking the sound,

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:51.670
flash, and recoil of a real weapon.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:53.360
And when a weapon uses a blank cartridge,

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:54.610
it can still be dangerous.

00:07:54.610 --> 00:07:56.670
To achieve the flash sound and recoil,

00:07:56.670 --> 00:07:58.670
blank cartridges are
filled with gunpowder.

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:00.500
They frequently contain something inside

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:02.180
to seal the powder in the case,

00:08:02.180 --> 00:08:04.800
usually paper, wood, or
plastic, called a wad.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.080
And if something is inside
of the prop gun barrel,

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:09.950
it will be expelled at nearly
the same velocity as a bullet.

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:11.830
So if a blank is
discharged at close range,

00:08:11.830 --> 00:08:13.760
the wad can penetrate the human body.

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:16.080
And this is what happened when
after Brandon Lee was killed

00:08:16.080 --> 00:08:18.380
on the set of 1994 film "The Crow"

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:21.080
when there was a dummy
round stuck in the barrel.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:23.080
The dummy round that was
used in another scene

00:08:23.080 --> 00:08:25.010
should have been removed
before filming a closeup

00:08:25.010 --> 00:08:26.130
involving the weapon.

00:08:26.130 --> 00:08:28.800
However, a .44 caliber
Smith and Wesson revolver

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:30.280
had been hand-loaded by someone

00:08:30.280 --> 00:08:31.770
who wasn't a firearms expert.

00:08:31.770 --> 00:08:33.310
The person removed the propelling powder,

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:35.880
but unknowingly left the
live primer in place.

00:08:35.880 --> 00:08:37.830
This caused the bullet to
be separated from the casing

00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:39.780
without enough energy for
it to exit the barrel.

00:08:39.780 --> 00:08:42.170
The gun wasn't checked before
it was used to film a closeup,

00:08:42.170 --> 00:08:45.100
and during the scene actor
Michael Massee pointed the gun

00:08:45.100 --> 00:08:46.680
at Lee, and when he pulled the trigger,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.440
the dummy was blown out
of the barrel killing Lee.

00:08:49.440 --> 00:08:52.220
And the cartridges may also
result in hot exploding gas

00:08:52.220 --> 00:08:53.960
to mimic a muzzle blast.

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:57.160
The gas can also serious
injuries if fired at close range.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:00.750
For example, in 1984, actor
Jon-Erik Hexum was filming

00:09:00.750 --> 00:09:02.810
the seventh episode of
the CBS series "Cover Up."

00:09:02.810 --> 00:09:05.320
Hexum was filming a scene where
his character loaded bullets

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:06.660
into a .44 Magnum,

00:09:06.660 --> 00:09:09.060
so he was given a
functioning gun and blanks.

00:09:09.060 --> 00:09:11.430
When Hexum was frustrated
by a filming delay,

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:12.760
he started playing with the gun.

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:15.220
Hexum had removed all but one blank.

00:09:15.220 --> 00:09:16.790
He put the revolver to his temple,

00:09:16.790 --> 00:09:18.720
pulled the trigger as if
playing Russian Roulette.

00:09:18.720 --> 00:09:21.100
And although the wadding
did not penetrate his skull,

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.380
the force of the blast
created by the hot gas

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:26.440
cracked his skull and Hexum
died from a brain hemorrhage.

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:27.930
And perhaps by now you've
seen the similarities

00:09:27.930 --> 00:09:29.470
between all three of these incidents.

00:09:29.470 --> 00:09:30.860
They all involve revolvers

00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:32.610
rather than semi-automatic pistols,

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.080
because the requirements
for an onscreen revolver

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:36.870
are different than other pistols.

00:09:36.870 --> 00:09:38.630
Now it's my understanding
that on a western film set

00:09:38.630 --> 00:09:41.260
like this, film crews
do use real revolvers,

00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:43.900
but they use several
different kinds of ammunition.

00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:44.940
The first is a blank

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:47.220
that contains a cartridge with gunpowder.

00:09:47.220 --> 00:09:50.590
It will shoot a flame out
when you shoot the gun,

00:09:50.590 --> 00:09:52.070
but it doesn't contain an actual bullet.

00:09:52.070 --> 00:09:54.690
The casing is crimps where
the bullet would otherwise go.

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:55.640
But since in a revolver

00:09:55.640 --> 00:09:57.330
the bullets are exposed to the camera,

00:09:57.330 --> 00:09:59.180
you need at least one other kind of round.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:00.510
That would be a dummy round

00:10:00.510 --> 00:10:02.790
where it does contain the actual bullet,

00:10:02.790 --> 00:10:06.130
the round lead thing, but it
doesn't contain any gunpowder.

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:09.570
And so when you depress the
hammer, the bullet doesn't fire

00:10:09.570 --> 00:10:11.680
because there's nothing to go off.

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:13.690
So at least with respect to
the one of the main criticisms

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:16.213
I've seen, why weren't
they just all blanks,

00:10:16.213 --> 00:10:18.550
blanks wouldn't look right on film.

00:10:18.550 --> 00:10:21.390
But since everyone knows how
dangerous prop guns can be,

00:10:21.390 --> 00:10:22.630
how could this happen?

00:10:22.630 --> 00:10:25.000
Should Baldwin have
checked the gun himself?

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:27.590
Well, given how complicated
it can be to film guns,

00:10:27.590 --> 00:10:29.870
it's not as simple as
discharging live rounds,

00:10:29.870 --> 00:10:33.060
it's imperative that firearms
experts check these weapons.

00:10:33.060 --> 00:10:34.570
It's also crucial for everyone on set

00:10:34.570 --> 00:10:35.930
to observe safety protocols.

00:10:35.930 --> 00:10:36.830
For example, in real life,

00:10:36.830 --> 00:10:39.020
you're never supposed to
point a gun at a person.

00:10:39.020 --> 00:10:40.660
But typically on the set of a film,

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:42.920
actors point the gun at
the camera all the time.

00:10:42.920 --> 00:10:44.640
But everyone in the line of fire

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:46.670
would be wearing goggles and face masks.

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:48.740
They also stand behind a Perspex screen.

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.670
Hutchins and Souza were
apparently checking camera angles

00:10:51.670 --> 00:10:53.510
by looking at the monitor
when they were struck

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:54.630
during the process.

00:10:54.630 --> 00:10:55.970
So this is a lot of background,

00:10:55.970 --> 00:10:57.330
but it's necessary to determine

00:10:57.330 --> 00:10:59.850
whether anyone faces
criminal or civil liability

00:10:59.850 --> 00:11:01.180
as a result of what happened.

00:11:01.180 --> 00:11:03.990
So let's talk about the
liability of the cast and crew,

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.090
and let's start with actor Alec Baldwin.

00:11:06.090 --> 00:11:07.610
When the shooting made headlines,

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:08.800
Baldwin's political opponents

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:10.590
immediately shared their thoughts.

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:12.540
And of course, it's natural
to point the finger at Baldwin

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:14.930
because he's the one that
physically pulled the trigger

00:11:14.930 --> 00:11:15.940
of the gun in this case.

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:17.860
But there's a difference
between an accidental shooting

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:19.990
on a movie set and an intentional killing.

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:21.840
New Mexico criminal law defines murder as

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:24.310
the killing of one human being by another

00:11:24.310 --> 00:11:26.340
without lawful justification or excuse.

00:11:26.340 --> 00:11:28.190
And we've talked about
the mental states required

00:11:28.190 --> 00:11:29.420
for murder in the first degree,

00:11:29.420 --> 00:11:31.340
which usually requires
malice aforethought,

00:11:31.340 --> 00:11:33.140
which means did the defendant
plan to commit murder

00:11:33.140 --> 00:11:34.500
without cause or provocation?

00:11:34.500 --> 00:11:36.667
Manslaughter, on the other
hand, is defined as the,

00:11:36.667 --> 00:11:38.920
"Unlawful killing of a
human being without malice."

00:11:38.920 --> 00:11:40.580
Because manslaughter does not involve

00:11:40.580 --> 00:11:42.200
the mental state of malice,

00:11:42.200 --> 00:11:43.932
it's considered less serious than murder.

00:11:43.932 --> 00:11:47.320
Malice aforethought is
a criminal term of art

00:11:47.320 --> 00:11:49.340
that I'm not going to go
into the nuances here,

00:11:49.340 --> 00:11:51.120
but it doesn't mean anger necessarily.

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:52.980
I'm going to assume that
Baldwin did not intend

00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:56.230
to hurt anyone here, so that
means murder is off the table.

00:11:56.230 --> 00:11:58.430
Now, New Mexico manslaughter
cases are classified

00:11:58.430 --> 00:12:00.740
as either involuntary or voluntary.

00:12:00.740 --> 00:12:02.530
Voluntary manslaughter involves a killing

00:12:02.530 --> 00:12:04.457
that occurs during, "A sudden quarrel

00:12:04.457 --> 00:12:05.740
"or in the heat of passion."

00:12:05.740 --> 00:12:07.330
As far as we know, that
doesn't apply to anyone

00:12:07.330 --> 00:12:09.180
on the "Rust" film set, including Baldwin.

00:12:09.180 --> 00:12:11.340
The crime of involuntary
manslaughter involves a killing

00:12:11.340 --> 00:12:14.220
that occurs in the commission
of some other unlawful act

00:12:14.220 --> 00:12:15.300
or a felony crime.

00:12:15.300 --> 00:12:18.080
Involuntary manslaughter also
involves a killing that occurs

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:20.150
while the defendant is
engaging in a lawful

00:12:20.150 --> 00:12:22.020
but dangerous act without exercising

00:12:22.020 --> 00:12:23.880
due caution and circumspection.

00:12:23.880 --> 00:12:25.780
And an example of an
involuntary manslaughter

00:12:25.780 --> 00:12:28.410
might be a fatal traffic
accident caused by a driver

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:30.580
who was texting while driving.

00:12:30.580 --> 00:12:32.090
And to prove involuntary manslaughter,

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:33.810
prosecutors must show three things.

00:12:33.810 --> 00:12:35.380
First, that someone was killed

00:12:35.380 --> 00:12:37.270
as a result of an act of the defendant;

00:12:37.270 --> 00:12:39.280
second, that the act
was inherently dangerous

00:12:39.280 --> 00:12:40.160
or done recklessly;

00:12:40.160 --> 00:12:41.900
and third, that the
defendant should have known

00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:43.920
the act threatened the lives of others.

00:12:43.920 --> 00:12:46.510
And prosecutors don't need
to prove intent in New Mexico

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:47.680
for involuntary manslaughter

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:49.660
because it can be committed recklessly

00:12:49.660 --> 00:12:52.320
or through an inherently dangerous act.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.380
And the primary defense to
involuntary manslaughter

00:12:54.380 --> 00:12:56.320
is that it was just an accident.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:58.900
A defendant can show that
they didn't act irresponsibly

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.170
or didn't know that their
action could have resulted

00:13:01.170 --> 00:13:02.350
in another person's death.

00:13:02.350 --> 00:13:04.930
So the question is, did
Baldwin act recklessly

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:07.640
or commit an inherently dangerous act?

00:13:07.640 --> 00:13:09.540
Did he violate standard safety procedures

00:13:09.540 --> 00:13:11.650
by pointing the gun directly at people?

00:13:11.650 --> 00:13:13.160
Well, the early reports
suggest that Baldwin

00:13:13.160 --> 00:13:15.910
didn't just pick up the gun and
start pointing it at people.

00:13:15.910 --> 00:13:17.510
According to the crew members who spoke

00:13:17.510 --> 00:13:18.667
to the Los Angeles Times,

00:13:18.667 --> 00:13:20.727
"Baldwin removed the gun from its holster

00:13:20.727 --> 00:13:21.697
"once without incident,

00:13:21.697 --> 00:13:22.837
"but the second time he did so,

00:13:22.837 --> 00:13:25.380
"ammunition flew towards the
trio around the monitor."

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:27.100
This suggests there was
something wrong with the weapon,

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.260
but shouldn't Baldwin have
checked the gun himself?

00:13:29.260 --> 00:13:33.250
Wouldn't anyone handling a
firearm check the gun themselves?

00:13:33.250 --> 00:13:35.890
Well, Mike Tristano, an armorer
who worked with Alec Baldwin

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:38.370
in the 1998 movie "Thick as Thieves,"

00:13:38.370 --> 00:13:41.597
said that, "He was great to
work with and was very safe.

00:13:41.597 --> 00:13:42.837
"I was surprised to hear it was him.

00:13:42.837 --> 00:13:44.787
"He was always on the mark
and very professional.

00:13:44.787 --> 00:13:45.900
"He's not stupid."

00:13:45.900 --> 00:13:48.990
Industry insiders seem to
indicate that it's totally normal

00:13:48.990 --> 00:13:51.000
for an actor to rely on
the rest of the film crew

00:13:51.000 --> 00:13:52.650
to properly handle the weapons,

00:13:52.650 --> 00:13:53.900
especially when some of the crew

00:13:53.900 --> 00:13:55.720
have explicitly told the actor

00:13:55.720 --> 00:13:58.430
that the gun is safe to be
used in the circumstances.

00:13:58.430 --> 00:14:00.330
And that probably makes sense

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:02.240
to delegate this responsibility.

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:04.940
Actors aren't necessarily
gonna know all the ins and outs

00:14:04.940 --> 00:14:05.870
of all the different firearms,

00:14:05.870 --> 00:14:08.900
especially when different
kinds of ammunition are used

00:14:08.900 --> 00:14:10.300
in the same scene.

00:14:10.300 --> 00:14:13.980
And of course, if actors did
have to recheck every firearm

00:14:13.980 --> 00:14:15.040
that they were given,

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:17.580
that could create even more uncertainty

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:19.790
and allow things to go
wrong in different ways

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:22.480
if they didn't reload
the weapon correctly.

00:14:22.480 --> 00:14:24.900
So, this sort of delegation,
in my mind at least,

00:14:24.900 --> 00:14:27.460
as an industry standard
seems like it makes sense.

00:14:27.460 --> 00:14:29.380
So under the scenario as we know it,

00:14:29.380 --> 00:14:30.590
for Baldwin to be charged,

00:14:30.590 --> 00:14:32.970
he'd probably have to know
that there was a live round

00:14:32.970 --> 00:14:34.370
in the gun ahead of time.

00:14:34.370 --> 00:14:37.110
A weapons expert said
once the weapon is loaded

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:39.200
it's impossible to tell what's inside.

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:41.450
And Tristano said that
it was not Baldwin's job

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:42.467
to check the gun:

00:14:42.467 --> 00:14:44.347
"Someone professional on set

00:14:44.347 --> 00:14:45.767
"should have been handling that gun.

00:14:45.767 --> 00:14:47.140
"This makes no sense."

00:14:47.140 --> 00:14:49.400
So then the only question is
whether Baldwin was negligent

00:14:49.400 --> 00:14:51.260
or reckless in handling the weapon,

00:14:51.260 --> 00:14:53.340
and it doesn't seem that we
have any facts to indicate that,

00:14:53.340 --> 00:14:55.720
for example, he was
playing around with the gun

00:14:55.720 --> 00:14:58.170
or doing things that
someone told him not to do,

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:00.040
which is probably what would be necessary.

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:01.550
So although Baldwin probably won't face

00:15:01.550 --> 00:15:02.420
any criminal charges,

00:15:02.420 --> 00:15:03.960
the likelihood that he is going to be sued

00:15:03.960 --> 00:15:06.440
as a producer of the movie
is probably pretty high.

00:15:06.440 --> 00:15:08.130
But it doesn't mean that
no one from the "Rust" film

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:09.780
will face criminal liability.

00:15:09.780 --> 00:15:11.920
Is it possible that Baldwin
or others could be charged

00:15:11.920 --> 00:15:14.400
with another crime like
negligent use of a deadly weapon?

00:15:14.400 --> 00:15:15.930
Well, negligent use of a deadly weapon

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:18.360
consists of, one, discharging
the firearm into any building

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.110
or vehicle or so as to
knowingly endanger a person

00:15:21.110 --> 00:15:21.960
or his property;

00:15:21.960 --> 00:15:24.000
two, carrying a firearm
while under the influence

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:25.640
of an intoxicant or narcotic;

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:27.150
three, endangering the safety of another

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:29.700
by handling or using a
firearm or other deadly weapon

00:15:29.700 --> 00:15:30.970
in a negligent manner;

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:32.490
or four, discharging a firearm

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:34.730
within 150 yards of a
dwelling or building.

00:15:34.730 --> 00:15:37.060
Now, Subsection 3 may
be in play in this case.

00:15:37.060 --> 00:15:39.170
An early report said that
the New Mexico police

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:40.650
were trying to determine whether the gun

00:15:40.650 --> 00:15:42.560
would be considered, "A deadly weapon."

00:15:42.560 --> 00:15:44.180
And based on what we know about prop guns

00:15:44.180 --> 00:15:45.300
and firing dummy rounds,

00:15:45.300 --> 00:15:46.820
and the fact that they may
have actually been using

00:15:46.820 --> 00:15:48.600
real guns with fake ammunition,

00:15:48.600 --> 00:15:51.380
one would think the answer
in this case could be yes.

00:15:51.380 --> 00:15:53.380
And some members of the
"Rust" crew have put the blame

00:15:53.380 --> 00:15:54.850
on the assistant director.

00:15:54.850 --> 00:15:56.627
A script assistant who called 911 said--

00:15:56.627 --> 00:15:57.460
- [Script Supervisor] This mother(beep).

00:15:57.460 --> 00:16:00.460
Did you see him get up in
my face and yell at me?

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:01.700
He's supposed to check the guns.

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:02.650
He's responsible for what happened.

00:16:02.650 --> 00:16:04.170
- And there were also prior complaints

00:16:04.170 --> 00:16:06.580
about unsafe working
conditions on the "Rust" set.

00:16:06.580 --> 00:16:08.740
On October 16th, the union
that represents people

00:16:08.740 --> 00:16:11.940
who work on theater and film
crews nearly went on strike

00:16:11.940 --> 00:16:13.480
over bad working conditions.

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:17.270
The IATSE crew cited low wages,
long hours, and unsafe sets.

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:19.300
The "Rust" crew said
that the set was unsafe,

00:16:19.300 --> 00:16:21.310
echoing many of the
concerns that IATSE had

00:16:21.310 --> 00:16:22.710
when it threatened to strike.

00:16:22.710 --> 00:16:24.070
And just hours before the shooting,

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:26.610
seven camera crew
members working on "Rust"

00:16:26.610 --> 00:16:29.840
walked off the set to protest
low pay and long hours.

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:31.600
Crew members had been told
that the producers would pay

00:16:31.600 --> 00:16:33.940
for them to stay in
hotel rooms in Santa Fe.

00:16:33.940 --> 00:16:34.930
But when they arrived on set,

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:36.670
they were told that they would
be staying in Albuquerque.

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:38.530
That's a one hour drive from the film set.

00:16:38.530 --> 00:16:40.790
That means that crew would
have to drive one hour to work,

00:16:40.790 --> 00:16:42.590
then be on set for 12 to 13 hours,

00:16:42.590 --> 00:16:45.130
and then drive for another
hour back to Albuquerque.

00:16:45.130 --> 00:16:47.280
They were allegedly only
getting five hours of sleep.

00:16:47.280 --> 00:16:49.270
The crew also said their
paychecks had been delayed.

00:16:49.270 --> 00:16:51.410
And three crew members told the LA Times

00:16:51.410 --> 00:16:52.870
that they were concerned with gun safety

00:16:52.870 --> 00:16:54.260
because there had already been

00:16:54.260 --> 00:16:57.127
two accidental prop gun discharges:

00:16:57.127 --> 00:16:58.377
"There were no safety meetings.

00:16:58.377 --> 00:17:00.907
"There was no assurance that
it wouldn't happen again.

00:17:00.907 --> 00:17:03.240
"All they wanted to do
was rush, rush, rush."

00:17:03.240 --> 00:17:05.360
One anonymous person said
that he was so alarmed

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:06.400
by the prop gun misfires,

00:17:06.400 --> 00:17:09.010
that he sent a text message to
the unit production manager.

00:17:09.010 --> 00:17:10.840
The LA Times reviewed a text message

00:17:10.840 --> 00:17:12.557
sent before the shooting that read,

00:17:12.557 --> 00:17:14.797
"We've now had three
accidental discharges.

00:17:14.797 --> 00:17:16.280
"This is super unsafe."

00:17:16.280 --> 00:17:18.900
Deadline cites an unnamed source
who said a gun had gone off

00:17:18.900 --> 00:17:20.987
in a cabin while someone was holding it.

00:17:20.987 --> 00:17:23.467
"A gun had two misfires in a closed cabin.

00:17:23.467 --> 00:17:24.877
"They just fired loud pops.

00:17:24.877 --> 00:17:26.567
"A person was just
holding it in their hands

00:17:26.567 --> 00:17:27.610
"and it went off."

00:17:27.610 --> 00:17:28.890
An anonymous crew member tweeted

00:17:28.890 --> 00:17:30.900
that the crew had sent a
message about gun safety

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:32.800
shortly before walking off the set.

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:35.120
Past and present crew
members have also spoken out

00:17:35.120 --> 00:17:37.340
against Assistant Director David Halls.

00:17:37.340 --> 00:17:39.460
In 2019, Halls was the assistant director

00:17:39.460 --> 00:17:41.160
for the movie "Freedom's Path."

00:17:41.160 --> 00:17:43.140
In that movie, a gun discharged,

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:44.400
which injured a crew member.

00:17:44.400 --> 00:17:45.720
The injured man was a boom operator

00:17:45.720 --> 00:17:47.290
who suffered inner ear injuries.

00:17:47.290 --> 00:17:49.870
Halls was fired and the
armorer was replaced.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:51.360
The "Freedom's Path" production company

00:17:51.360 --> 00:17:52.547
confirmed the incident:

00:17:52.547 --> 00:17:54.077
"Halls was removed from
the set immediately

00:17:54.077 --> 00:17:55.427
"after the prop gun discharged.

00:17:55.427 --> 00:17:58.080
"Production did not resume
filming until Dave was off site."

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.190
Now, however, the
anonymous crew members said

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:02.830
that there was no armorer
on "Freedom's Path,"

00:18:02.830 --> 00:18:04.580
that Halls let a background actor

00:18:04.580 --> 00:18:06.130
take control of the prop gun,

00:18:06.130 --> 00:18:07.460
that there was no safety meeting,

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:09.030
and that several people asked Halls

00:18:09.030 --> 00:18:12.070
if the weapon had been cleared
of ammunition and gunpowder,

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:13.240
but Halls didn't say.

00:18:13.240 --> 00:18:14.730
The "Freedom's Path" producers denied

00:18:14.730 --> 00:18:15.940
that part of the story.

00:18:15.940 --> 00:18:17.480
And now we've heard
about several incidents

00:18:17.480 --> 00:18:19.900
involving gun safety and David Halls.

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:21.890
These incidents could clearly be relevant

00:18:21.890 --> 00:18:23.680
towards civil liability to the producers

00:18:23.680 --> 00:18:24.950
who continued to hire Halls

00:18:24.950 --> 00:18:27.450
and might have had notice
of his past behavior.

00:18:27.450 --> 00:18:28.960
Now, producers from three productions

00:18:28.960 --> 00:18:31.180
involving Assistant
Director Halls have issued

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:32.160
carefully worded statements

00:18:32.160 --> 00:18:34.010
about what happened on their sets.

00:18:34.010 --> 00:18:35.730
That's because they
might have legal exposure

00:18:35.730 --> 00:18:38.350
for injuries that happened
on their projects too.

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:41.230
The cause of action against
producers would be negligence.

00:18:41.230 --> 00:18:44.050
The New Mexico Wrongful Death
Act allows monetary recovery

00:18:44.050 --> 00:18:46.950
for a death that occurs as a
result of someone's negligence.

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:49.010
The statute provides that
parties seeking recovery

00:18:49.010 --> 00:18:50.040
can pursue an action

00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:51.510
if legal recourse would
have been available

00:18:51.510 --> 00:18:52.525
for a personal injury.

00:18:52.525 --> 00:18:54.590
And this is something that
people get wrong all the time.

00:18:54.590 --> 00:18:55.985
When someone passes away,

00:18:55.985 --> 00:18:58.940
the legal cause of action
just doesn't go away.

00:18:58.940 --> 00:19:01.970
The estate can sue on behalf
of the deceased person.

00:19:01.970 --> 00:19:04.270
Having actual knowledge
of lax safety protocols

00:19:04.270 --> 00:19:07.560
and actual knowledge of prior misfires

00:19:07.560 --> 00:19:09.880
is the kind of thing that takes negligence

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:11.880
to actual recklessness,

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:15.320
and might go from civil
liability to criminal liability.

00:19:15.320 --> 00:19:17.170
Firearms experts told Variety

00:19:17.170 --> 00:19:18.520
that actors are given fake guns

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:19.890
until it's time to shoot the scene.

00:19:19.890 --> 00:19:23.217
When it's time for action, the
actor is handed the real gun:

00:19:23.217 --> 00:19:26.157
"It is understood that when
the firearm is handed to him,

00:19:26.157 --> 00:19:27.687
"it is in proper working order.

00:19:27.687 --> 00:19:30.247
"And that is a responsibility
of the armorer or prop master,

00:19:30.247 --> 00:19:32.560
"whoever is in control
of the firearms on set."

00:19:32.560 --> 00:19:33.830
If there were prior incidents

00:19:33.830 --> 00:19:36.050
of accidental weapons'
discharge on the "Rust" set

00:19:36.050 --> 00:19:37.500
and the assistant director knew about them

00:19:37.500 --> 00:19:38.333
but didn't correct them

00:19:38.333 --> 00:19:40.200
or insist on greater safety protocols,

00:19:40.200 --> 00:19:41.510
it's possible that he could be charged

00:19:41.510 --> 00:19:43.040
with involuntary manslaughter.

00:19:43.040 --> 00:19:45.050
Prosecutors would need to
prove that the staff committed

00:19:45.050 --> 00:19:48.630
a lawful but dangerous act,
such as handling a prop gun,

00:19:48.630 --> 00:19:50.480
and did not act with due caution.

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:52.930
Now remember, to be convicted
in the state of New Mexico,

00:19:52.930 --> 00:19:55.187
the prosecutor needs only
to prove that someone was,

00:19:55.187 --> 00:19:57.297
"Endangering the safety
of others by handling

00:19:57.297 --> 00:19:59.157
"or using a firearm or other deadly weapon

00:19:59.157 --> 00:20:00.480
"in a negligent manner."

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:02.070
And that takes us to the
person that's responsible

00:20:02.070 --> 00:20:04.670
for maintaining the weapons
on the film set: the armorer.

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:06.190
The armorer on "Rust" was the daughter

00:20:06.190 --> 00:20:08.190
of a legendary Hollywood armorer.

00:20:08.190 --> 00:20:10.747
And last month the
armorer told the podcast

00:20:10.747 --> 00:20:12.730
"Voices of the West" that
she had just completed

00:20:12.730 --> 00:20:15.460
her first film, a western
starring Nicolas Cage.

00:20:15.460 --> 00:20:18.600
Her job on "Rust" was just
her second as lead armorer.

00:20:18.600 --> 00:20:20.770
She said that it's standard
to assume that no one on set,

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:21.870
from the actors to the crew,

00:20:21.870 --> 00:20:23.970
had ever handled a weapon before.

00:20:23.970 --> 00:20:26.220
And that person explained
that her philosophy

00:20:26.220 --> 00:20:28.690
was that films should always use blanks.

00:20:28.690 --> 00:20:29.700
However, she said--

00:20:29.700 --> 00:20:31.350
- [Hannah] Some people really
don't want you to dummy it

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:35.422
for some reason, just out
of their own, I don't know,

00:20:35.422 --> 00:20:37.240
how they want to feel
comfortable, I guess.

00:20:37.240 --> 00:20:38.740
- She could have been
referring to the producers,

00:20:38.740 --> 00:20:40.740
directors, or other prop masters.

00:20:40.740 --> 00:20:44.100
We don't know if anyone decided
to use live rounds on set.

00:20:44.100 --> 00:20:45.100
The armorer said, quote--

00:20:45.100 --> 00:20:46.100
- [Hannah] I think loading blanks

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:48.520
was like the scariest thing to me,

00:20:48.520 --> 00:20:51.550
because I was like, "Oh, I
don't know anything about it."

00:20:51.550 --> 00:20:52.980
But, you know, he taught me that,

00:20:52.980 --> 00:20:55.700
and eventually by the time I
was like trying to figure out

00:20:55.700 --> 00:20:59.420
how to make a specific blank
go when you want it to,

00:20:59.420 --> 00:21:01.670
rather than it hitting
like the empty cylinders

00:21:01.670 --> 00:21:05.350
and everything, figured
that out on my own.

00:21:05.350 --> 00:21:06.900
- [Host] Explain that process.

00:21:06.900 --> 00:21:09.060
- [Hannah] So normally,
if you, like, you know,

00:21:09.060 --> 00:21:11.410
you open the loading gate,
you put the bullet in,

00:21:11.410 --> 00:21:15.580
you have to put it right
around to right before...

00:21:15.580 --> 00:21:18.160
You have to bring that bullet, that blank,

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:20.550
all the way around to
right before the cylinder,

00:21:20.550 --> 00:21:23.770
so then that way the next time
someone pulls back the hammer

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:25.963
and shoots it, it'll go off.

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.510
Yeah, and you have to, like,
look at the front of it

00:21:29.510 --> 00:21:31.050
and determine which one is the blank

00:21:31.050 --> 00:21:32.590
if it's dummied up, you know?

00:21:32.590 --> 00:21:34.169
That's how I tell, at least.

00:21:34.169 --> 00:21:35.620
- The podcaster asked if
the rest of the cylinder

00:21:35.620 --> 00:21:37.420
is generally filled with dummy wads,

00:21:37.420 --> 00:21:40.010
and the armorer said that usually it was.

00:21:40.010 --> 00:21:42.910
In a video of actor Jensen
Ackles discussed the gun training

00:21:42.910 --> 00:21:44.250
that he received for the film.

00:21:44.250 --> 00:21:46.800
Ackles talked about
gun safety on the movie

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:49.430
during a convention for fans
of the TV show "Supernatural."

00:21:49.430 --> 00:21:50.263
Ackles said--

00:21:50.263 --> 00:21:52.110
- They had me pick my gun.

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:55.702
They're like, "All right,
what gun would you like?"

00:21:55.702 --> 00:21:56.857
And I was like, "I don't know."

00:21:56.857 --> 00:21:59.437
And the armorer was like,
"Do you have gun experience?"

00:21:59.437 --> 00:22:01.233
(audience laughing)

00:22:01.233 --> 00:22:02.306
- Like, what's a gun?

00:22:02.306 --> 00:22:03.955
(audience laughing)

00:22:03.955 --> 00:22:06.580
- And I was like, "A little."

00:22:06.580 --> 00:22:08.840
And she's like, "Okay, well,
this is how you load it.

00:22:08.840 --> 00:22:10.017
(audience laughing)

00:22:10.017 --> 00:22:12.610
"This is how we check it
and make sure it's safe."

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:13.443
I'm like, "Okay."

00:22:13.443 --> 00:22:14.799
She's like, "Okay, I'm gonna
put some blanks in there

00:22:14.799 --> 00:22:16.037
"and I want you to just go over

00:22:16.037 --> 00:22:18.370
"and fire off a couple of
rounds towards the hill."

00:22:18.370 --> 00:22:19.203
And I was like, "Okay."

00:22:19.203 --> 00:22:21.350
So I put the belt on, I put the gun there,

00:22:21.350 --> 00:22:23.617
and I walked out, and she's
like, "Just make sure that you,

00:22:23.617 --> 00:22:25.447
"you know, you pull the
hammer all the way back

00:22:25.447 --> 00:22:26.850
"and you aim at your target."

00:22:26.850 --> 00:22:28.837
And I was like, "Okay, I got it."

00:22:28.837 --> 00:22:29.987
And then I go (firing).

00:22:31.885 --> 00:22:32.718
(audience laughing)

00:22:32.718 --> 00:22:33.900
- This statement might be relevant

00:22:33.900 --> 00:22:34.890
to the police investigation,

00:22:34.890 --> 00:22:36.840
as well as the investigation
by the state agency

00:22:36.840 --> 00:22:38.730
which investigates workplace accidents.

00:22:38.730 --> 00:22:40.210
He said the gun was loaded with blanks

00:22:40.210 --> 00:22:41.730
and the armorer told the podcaster

00:22:41.730 --> 00:22:43.580
that that was her strong preference.

00:22:43.580 --> 00:22:45.130
This video might also be highly relevant

00:22:45.130 --> 00:22:46.880
to the amount of training or lack thereof

00:22:46.880 --> 00:22:48.470
that the actors and staff received

00:22:48.470 --> 00:22:49.730
when they were working on this film.

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:51.250
There were also reports
that the crew had concerns

00:22:51.250 --> 00:22:52.890
that Hannah Gutierrez-Reed

00:22:52.890 --> 00:22:55.230
didn't have the necessary expertise.

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:57.410
Unit production manager Katherine Walters

00:22:57.410 --> 00:22:58.950
sent a Slack message on October 8th.

00:22:58.950 --> 00:23:02.160
It said, "Apparently props and
armor require handholding."

00:23:02.160 --> 00:23:03.300
They claimed that there were other

00:23:03.300 --> 00:23:04.870
accidental discharges on set.

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:06.910
First, Baldwin's stunt
double had accidentally

00:23:06.910 --> 00:23:09.370
fired a blank after being
told that his gun was cold.

00:23:09.370 --> 00:23:11.240
Reed denies that she told the stuntman

00:23:11.240 --> 00:23:12.300
that the gun was cold.

00:23:12.300 --> 00:23:13.670
She claims that she told the man

00:23:13.670 --> 00:23:15.400
that the gun was hot with blanks.

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:17.577
Second, "A young woman
from the props department

00:23:17.577 --> 00:23:19.570
"actually shot herself in the foot."

00:23:19.570 --> 00:23:20.760
That round was a blank.

00:23:20.760 --> 00:23:22.410
And following the press
conference by police,

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:24.550
Hannah Gutierrez-Reed
released her first statement

00:23:24.550 --> 00:23:25.383
about the shooting.

00:23:25.383 --> 00:23:26.510
According to her lawyers,

00:23:26.510 --> 00:23:28.280
the producers hired her for two roles:

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:29.960
armorer and prop assistant.

00:23:29.960 --> 00:23:31.880
She said being the prop
assistant made it difficult

00:23:31.880 --> 00:23:34.010
for her to do her job as armorer.

00:23:34.010 --> 00:23:35.760
She said that she requested more time

00:23:35.760 --> 00:23:38.260
for training the actors,
and this was denied.

00:23:38.260 --> 00:23:39.380
She also said that both she

00:23:39.380 --> 00:23:42.220
and the prop master Sarah
Zachary had complete control

00:23:42.220 --> 00:23:43.130
over the guns.

00:23:43.130 --> 00:23:45.690
Reed told investigators that
on the day of the shooting,

00:23:45.690 --> 00:23:47.390
during lunch the firearms were secured

00:23:47.390 --> 00:23:48.760
in a safe with a combination

00:23:48.760 --> 00:23:52.210
on a white prop truck that only
a few people had access to.

00:23:52.210 --> 00:23:54.490
But the ammunition was
left on an unsecured cart

00:23:54.490 --> 00:23:56.750
and wasn't in a separate
locked compartment.

00:23:56.750 --> 00:23:59.630
Reed was adamant that there
was no live ammo on set.

00:23:59.630 --> 00:24:02.617
She said she, "Never witnessed
anyone shoot live rounds

00:24:02.617 --> 00:24:05.220
"with these guns, nor
would she permit that."

00:24:05.220 --> 00:24:07.960
After lunch, Reed said that
Zachary got the firearms

00:24:07.960 --> 00:24:08.820
from the safe.

00:24:08.820 --> 00:24:11.917
During filming, she,
"Gave the gun to Baldwin

00:24:11.917 --> 00:24:12.977
"a couple of times,

00:24:12.977 --> 00:24:15.480
"as well as to First Assistant
Director David Halls."

00:24:15.480 --> 00:24:17.207
Reed denied that any of the weapons were,

00:24:17.207 --> 00:24:19.780
"Unaccounted for or being
shot by crew members."

00:24:19.780 --> 00:24:22.050
According to Reed, she
locked up the set's guns

00:24:22.050 --> 00:24:23.420
at night and at lunch.

00:24:23.420 --> 00:24:25.490
Serge Svetnoy was a crew member on "Rust."

00:24:25.490 --> 00:24:27.767
He wrote a Facebook post
blaming Hutchins's death on,

00:24:27.767 --> 00:24:29.610
"Negligence and unprofessionalism."

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:31.220
Although he didn't name the armorer,

00:24:31.220 --> 00:24:32.917
Svetnoy clearly referred to her:

00:24:32.917 --> 00:24:34.557
"I'm sure that we had the professionals

00:24:34.557 --> 00:24:36.207
"in every department, but one,

00:24:36.207 --> 00:24:38.327
"the department that was
responsible for the weapons.

00:24:38.327 --> 00:24:39.977
"There's no way that a 24-year-old woman

00:24:39.977 --> 00:24:41.817
"can be a professional with armory.

00:24:41.817 --> 00:24:44.307
"Professionals are the people
who have spent years on sets,

00:24:44.307 --> 00:24:46.427
"people who know this job from A to Z.

00:24:46.427 --> 00:24:48.727
"These are the people who
have the safety on set

00:24:48.727 --> 00:24:50.357
"at the level of reflexes.

00:24:50.357 --> 00:24:53.967
"They do not need to be told
to put the sandbag on a tripod,

00:24:53.967 --> 00:24:56.817
"fix the ladder on stage, or
fence off the explosion site.

00:24:56.817 --> 00:24:57.947
"They have it in their blood.

00:24:57.947 --> 00:25:00.083
"I'm calling out to the producers."

00:25:00.083 --> 00:25:01.910
Svetnoy said producers cut corners

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:03.670
by hiring unqualified
people to save money.

00:25:03.670 --> 00:25:06.470
And the producers obviously
pushed back on that claim.

00:25:06.470 --> 00:25:08.150
Because it's not just the
people that are on set

00:25:08.150 --> 00:25:10.300
that might face civil
or criminal liability

00:25:10.300 --> 00:25:12.160
as a result of what happened.

00:25:12.160 --> 00:25:14.600
It's possible that the
producers of the movie

00:25:14.600 --> 00:25:17.110
might face vicarious
liability for the negligence

00:25:17.110 --> 00:25:18.870
that may or may not have happened on set

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:20.680
or potentially negligently hiring people

00:25:20.680 --> 00:25:23.200
who weren't capable of
delivering the safety

00:25:23.200 --> 00:25:24.190
that was required.

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:26.140
And not surprisingly, the
use of firearms on set

00:25:26.140 --> 00:25:28.510
involves extra layers of
disclosure and planning.

00:25:28.510 --> 00:25:30.320
Producers usually have
to submit their plans

00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:33.350
for using firearms on set for
review by insurance officials

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:35.510
or else it's hard to get
a bond for the production.

00:25:35.510 --> 00:25:38.010
For liability purposes,
Baldwin was probably entitled

00:25:38.010 --> 00:25:40.890
to rely on the assistant
director yelling, "Cold gun,"

00:25:40.890 --> 00:25:42.890
but new details about
the shooting indicate

00:25:42.890 --> 00:25:45.210
that we can't totally
rule out homicide charges

00:25:45.210 --> 00:25:47.130
against the armorer
and assistant director.

00:25:47.130 --> 00:25:48.330
Prosecutors would still need to show

00:25:48.330 --> 00:25:50.330
that the responsible
person had a deprived mind,

00:25:50.330 --> 00:25:52.460
and in practice, this occurs when a person

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:55.060
intentionally engages in
outrageous, reckless conduct

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:57.360
with a total indifference
to the value of human life.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:00.460
However, mere negligence or
recklessness isn't enough.

00:26:00.460 --> 00:26:02.780
The prosecutor must prove that
the person knew their conduct

00:26:02.780 --> 00:26:05.420
was greatly dangerous
to the lives of others.

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:06.930
An example would be a person shooting

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:08.290
into a crowd of people.

00:26:08.290 --> 00:26:10.630
So, if live ammunition
was deliberately loaded

00:26:10.630 --> 00:26:12.410
into the firearm that
was handed to Baldwin

00:26:12.410 --> 00:26:14.930
by a person who knew the gun
would be pointed at others,

00:26:14.930 --> 00:26:16.590
then that could be a murder case.

00:26:16.590 --> 00:26:18.080
But more likely what we're looking at here

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:21.880
is a case of manslaughter,
whether voluntary or involuntary,

00:26:21.880 --> 00:26:24.370
because it looks like someone just engaged

00:26:24.370 --> 00:26:28.030
in manifest negligence
or extreme recklessness.

00:26:28.030 --> 00:26:30.000
So, detectives will also
take into consideration

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:32.560
whether there really were
two accidental discharges

00:26:32.560 --> 00:26:33.420
before the shooting,

00:26:33.420 --> 00:26:36.620
because that speaks to
very, very lax protocols.

00:26:36.620 --> 00:26:37.610
And if that turns out to be true,

00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:39.530
that there were many
accidental discharges,

00:26:39.530 --> 00:26:41.160
which should never happen,

00:26:41.160 --> 00:26:44.120
and the assistant director or
the armorer knew about this

00:26:44.120 --> 00:26:45.810
because they were responsible for it,

00:26:45.810 --> 00:26:47.970
then they might have some
serious criminal liability,

00:26:47.970 --> 00:26:49.930
probably just not a homicide.

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:52.360
And Brandon Lee's family settled a lawsuit

00:26:52.360 --> 00:26:53.840
against producers of "The Crow."

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:55.940
The death of camera assistant Sarah Jones

00:26:55.940 --> 00:26:58.380
in a train accident also
resulted in a lawsuit,

00:26:58.380 --> 00:27:01.420
in addition to new protocols
for filming on railroad tracks.

00:27:01.420 --> 00:27:04.120
I'm sure all of this will
come out in an investigation,

00:27:04.120 --> 00:27:07.520
but plaintiffs will want to
know who knew what and when.

00:27:07.520 --> 00:27:09.820
Were people aware that
the crew had walked out,

00:27:09.820 --> 00:27:11.710
that they had complained
about safety issues?

00:27:11.710 --> 00:27:14.320
Did people know about the
other accidental discharges,

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:15.530
if those actually happened?

00:27:15.530 --> 00:27:16.910
Did people talk to anyone?

00:27:16.910 --> 00:27:18.620
Did they make any changes in protocol?

00:27:18.620 --> 00:27:21.310
There've been thousands, if not
tens of thousands of movies,

00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:23.760
that have used firearms since
the Brandon Lee incident

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:26.320
in the '90s, and none of them, until now,

00:27:26.320 --> 00:27:28.370
had resulted in someone dying.

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:29.590
Now, there's still a
ton that we don't know

00:27:29.590 --> 00:27:31.470
about this incident, but
it's entirely possible

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:33.150
that someone is going to jail over this.

00:27:33.150 --> 00:27:36.110
And someone is certainly
going to pay a lot of money.

00:27:36.110 --> 00:27:37.610
But this video isn't over.

00:27:37.610 --> 00:27:39.660
There is an extended discussion
of the Baldwin incident

00:27:39.660 --> 00:27:42.670
over on Nebula, thanks to
today's sponsor Curiosity Stream.

00:27:42.670 --> 00:27:45.867
And I'm thrilled to announce
my first Nebula original,

00:27:45.867 --> 00:27:47.030
"Bad Law Words Good."

00:27:47.030 --> 00:27:49.240
It's a long form
documentary where I explore

00:27:49.240 --> 00:27:51.760
the most common legal
and linguistic comments

00:27:51.760 --> 00:27:54.170
that people throw around in everyday life,

00:27:54.170 --> 00:27:56.100
though they do it extremely incorrectly.

00:27:56.100 --> 00:27:58.700
So strap in, 'cause it's
gonna get really pedantic.

00:27:58.700 --> 00:28:01.180
It's a hilarious romp through
legal misunderstandings,

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:03.970
logical fallacies, and
"Law and Order" parodies.

00:28:03.970 --> 00:28:05.970
